Q: Build a simple SPA using Node.js + Express A: <p>Here's how to implement a minimal SPA, conceptually:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Serve a single index.html page regardless of which non-API route/resource the user asks for. For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Request: <code>https://www.example.com/foo</code> -&gt; serve index.html</li>
<li>Request: <code>https://www.example.com/todos/42</code> -&gt; serve index.html</li>
<li>Request: <code>https://www.example.com/todos?id=42</code> -&gt; serve index.html</li>
<li><em>...any non-API request</em> -&gt; serve index.html</li>
</ul>
<p>This is accomplished using a splat pattern in Express, shown below.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Have the single index.html page load relevant assets, most notably, .js files. JS handles client-side routing by parsing the pathname and query params from the URL to determine which page/resource the user is asking for, making calls to the backend to get data, and rendering the requested view.</p>
<p>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54231533/how-to-create-a-vanilla-js-routing-for-spa/67041356#67041356">How to create a vanilla JS routing for SPA?</a> for strategies and minimal examples.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Use JS to intercept and handle all user interactions with the page, mostly clicks and form submissions. For each interaction, make a call to the server's JSON API to get or post data, and then trigger navigations and re-render the page with <code>.innerHTML</code> as necessary.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://github.com/ggorlen/vanillaspa/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">This repo</a> exapnds the minimal front-end examples above to add a component system, and adds a pure Node backend to serve the site, but doesn't include a JSON API.</p>
<p>Adapting the minimal SPA examples to Express is much easier than vanilla Node. The main idea is to remove the templating engine, and then add a catch-all route for non-API calls to serve up the index.html that drives the entire front end.</p>
<p>For example, here's server.js:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const express = require(&quot;express&quot;);
const path = require(&quot;path&quot;);

const app = express();
const publicDir = path.join(__dirname, &quot;public&quot;);

app.use(express.static(publicDir));

// JSON API endpoints; this is a simple mock
app.get(&quot;/api/todos&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {
  res.json({
    data: [{id: 1, name: &quot;do the laundry&quot;}, {id: 2, name: &quot;learn to code&quot;}]
  });
});

// Serve index.html for any non-API requests
app.get(&quot;*splat&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {
  res.sendFile(path.join(publicDir, &quot;index.html&quot;));
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5001;
app.listen(PORT, () =&gt; {
  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
</code></pre>
<p>This is actually more less the same approach even if you're using a library like React, which will ultimately bundle down to a index.html and .js, .css and other asset files.</p>
<p>Here's a front end that builds on the linked history API example, adding a rudimentary component system and dynamic requests to the API route:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id=&quot;app&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;script&gt;
      const nav = `&lt;a href=&quot;/&quot;&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt; | 
                   &lt;a href=&quot;/about&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt; | 
                   &lt;a href=&quot;/todos&quot;&gt;TODOs&lt;/a&gt;`;

      const routes = {
        &quot;&quot;: {
          html: `&lt;h1&gt;Home&lt;/h1&gt;${nav}&lt;p&gt;Welcome home!&lt;/p&gt;`,
        },
        about: {
          html: `&lt;h1&gt;About&lt;/h1&gt;${nav}&lt;p&gt;This is a tiny SPA&lt;/p&gt;`,
        },
        todos: {
          html: `&lt;h1&gt;TODOs&lt;/h1&gt;${nav}&lt;ul id=&quot;todos&quot;&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;`,
          js: () =&gt; {
            fetch(&quot;/api/todos&quot;)
              .then((res) =&gt; res.json())
              .then(({ data }) =&gt; {
                const todos = document.querySelector(&quot;#todos&quot;);

                if (todos) {
                  todos.innerHTML = data
                    .map((e) =&gt; `&lt;li&gt;${e.name}&lt;/li&gt;`) // TODO: sanitize
                    .join(&quot;&quot;);
                }
              });
          },
        },
      };

      const render = (path) =&gt; {
        path = path.replace(/^\//, &quot;&quot;);
        const component = routes[path];
        console.log(path, component);
        document.querySelector(&quot;#app&quot;).innerHTML =
          component?.html ?? `&lt;h1&gt;404&lt;/h1&gt;${nav}`;
        document.querySelectorAll('#app [href^=&quot;/&quot;]').forEach((el) =&gt;
          el.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, (evt) =&gt; {
            evt.preventDefault();
            const { pathname: path } = new URL(evt.target.href);
            window.history.pushState({ path }, path, path);
            render(path);
          }),
        );
        component?.js?.();
      };

      window.addEventListener(&quot;popstate&quot;, (e) =&gt;
        render(new URL(window.location.href).pathname),
      );
      render(window.location.pathname);
    &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that as you add components, the motivation for React becomes pretty apparent, since you're basically reinventing the wheel.</p>
<p>For completeness, here's a package.json that works with the above code:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    &quot;express&quot;: &quot;^5.2.1&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>

